1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,821
This is ECE 358, video 2 b. The topic is the network core, part 2. Video 2 a explained forwarding, store and forward, queueing, and circuit switching.

2
00:00:13,271 --> 00:00:34,157
After this video, you can count the users that a link supports with circuit and packet switching. You can compare the two methods. And you can describe the structure of the Internet. At the end, we solve question bank questions 22 to 24 and 26 to 31, and problems 2 and 4 of problem set 1.

3
00:00:34,607 --> 00:00:48,409
Slide 28 compares the two methods with numbers. A 1 gigabit per second link is shared. Each user sends at 100 megabits per second when active, and is active 10 percent of the time.

4
00:00:48,859 --> 00:01:03,537
With circuit switching, each user reserves 100 megabits per second all the time. 1 gigabit divided by 100 megabits is 10. So only 10 users fit. User 11 gets no circuit.

5
00:01:03,987 --> 00:01:25,213
With packet switching, nobody reserves anything. Suppose 35 users share the link. On average, 35 times 0.1, which is 3.5 users, are active. In this moment, 4 users are active. They need 400 megabits per second. The link has enough capacity.

6
00:01:25,663 --> 00:01:41,087
The link is overloaded only when more than 10 users are active at the same time. The slide says this probability is less than point 0 0 0 4. So packet switching supports many more than 10 users.

7
00:01:41,537 --> 00:02:06,775
This slide is optional. The slide calls the calculation a homework problem. But problem set 1 uses the same method, so learn it. Each user is active with probability p. The users are independent. The probability that exactly n of M users are active is M choose n, times p to the power n, times 1 minus p to the power M minus n.

8
00:02:07,225 --> 00:02:17,230
Here M is 35 and p is 0.1. The bars show P of n. Most of the probability is near 3 or 4 active users.

9
00:02:17,680 --> 00:02:26,917
Overload means more than 10 active users. Add P of n for n from 11 to 35. The bars are too small to see.

10
00:02:27,367 --> 00:02:45,245
The sum is about 0.00042. So the link is overloaded only about 0.04 percent of the time. The slide gives the value as less than point 0 0 0 4. The exact sum is very close to it.

11
00:02:45,695 --> 00:02:56,468
Is packet switching always better? Data is often bursty. Sometimes a user has data to send, but at other times not. Here are three bursty users.

12
00:02:56,918 --> 00:03:14,881
Packet switching is great for bursty data. All users share the buffer and the link capacity. It is also simpler. There is no call setup, so no extra messages go before the data. This link carries 2 users, and most of the time the load is 2 or less.

13
00:03:15,331 --> 00:03:31,971
But excessive congestion is possible. In this slot, all 3 users are active. Packets queue, and buffer overflow causes delay and loss. So packet switching needs protocols for reliable data transfer and congestion control.

14
00:03:32,421 --> 00:03:41,167
How can packet switching give circuit-like behavior? The slide says: it is complicated. The course studies some techniques later.

15
00:03:41,617 --> 00:04:07,985
Now the structure of the Internet. Hosts connect to the Internet through access Internet Service Providers, or access I S Ps. The access I S Ps must be interconnected, so that any two hosts, anywhere, can send packets to each other. Economics and national policies drive the result. The slides build the structure step by step. There are millions of access I S Ps. How do we connect them?

16
00:04:08,435 --> 00:04:27,422
Option 1: connect each access I S P directly to each other access I S P. Each one needs a link to the other N minus 1. The number of links is N times N minus 1, divided by 2. For 8 access I S Ps, that is 28 links.

17
00:04:27,872 --> 00:04:39,776
For a million access I S Ps, it is about 5 times 10 to the power 11 links. The number grows as N squared. So the direct design does not scale.

18
00:04:40,226 --> 00:04:56,141
Option 2: connect each access I S P to one global transit I S P. Each access I S P needs only one link. So the number of links is N. For 8 access I S Ps, that is 8 links, not 28.

19
00:04:56,591 --> 00:05:06,063
The access I S P is the customer. The global I S P is the provider. The customer and the provider have an economic agreement.

20
00:05:06,513 --> 00:05:22,705
If one global I S P is a viable business, there will be competitors. Here are I S P A, B and C. Each one has its own access networks. But a host of I S P A cannot reach a host of I S P B yet.

21
00:05:23,155 --> 00:05:34,483
The competitors want to be connected. One way is an Internet exchange point, I X P. An I X P is a place where many I S Ps connect to each other.

22
00:05:34,933 --> 00:05:41,205
The second way is a peering link. A peering link is a direct link between two I S Ps.

23
00:05:41,655 --> 00:05:51,255
Next, regional networks may arise. A regional I S P connects the access networks of one region to a larger I S P.

24
00:05:51,705 --> 00:06:07,449
Last, content provider networks, for example Google, Microsoft and Akamai, may run their own network. It brings services and content close to the end users. It links directly to I X Ps and access networks.

25
00:06:07,899 --> 00:06:30,619
Here is the result. At the center is a small number of well-connected, large networks. The first kind is the tier-1 commercial I S P. Examples are Level 3, Sprint, AT and T, N T T, Orange and Tata Communications. They have national and international coverage. Tier-1 I S Ps connect to each other.

26
00:06:31,069 --> 00:06:34,866
Below them are the I X Ps. Many networks meet there.

27
00:06:35,316 --> 00:06:42,676
Next are the regional I S Ps. At the bottom are the access I S Ps, which connect the hosts.

28
00:06:43,126 --> 00:06:59,275
The second kind of network at the center is the content provider network, for example Google or Facebook. It is a private network that connects its data centers to the Internet. It often bypasses the tier-1 and the regional I S Ps.

29
00:06:59,725 --> 00:07:13,571
Slide 39 shows a real tier-1 network: Tata Communications. Its backbone links connect many continents. This is the national and international coverage of a tier-1 I S P.

30
00:07:14,021 --> 00:07:28,613
The backbone has more than 1500 gigabits per second of capacity. That is 1.5 times 10 to the power 12 bits per second. It carries more than 750 petabits each month.

31
00:07:29,063 --> 00:07:33,457
Questions 22 to 24. Pause the video and try them.

32
00:07:33,907 --> 00:07:42,462
Question 22. Circuit switching reserves buffer, link bandwidth, and processing power on the network elements of the path.

33
00:07:42,912 --> 00:08:10,859
Question 23. Circuit switching needs a call setup. Packet switching needs no call setup. Circuit switching reserves dedicated resources. Packet switching shares them. Circuit switching gives guaranteed performance. Packet switching can have delay and loss from congestion. And circuit switching supports a fixed number of users, 10 on the 1 gigabit link. Packet switching supports a variable number, many more than 10.

34
00:08:11,309 --> 00:08:22,679
Question 24. The present-day Internet behaves like a packet switched network. Hosts break messages into packets, and routers forward them with no call setup.

35
00:08:23,129 --> 00:08:27,417
Questions 26 to 28. Pause the video and try them.

36
00:08:27,867 --> 00:08:37,937
Question 26. An I S P is a provider of a service: Internet access. Hosts connect to the Internet through access I S Ps.

37
00:08:38,387 --> 00:09:01,640
Question 27. A tier-1 I S P is a large commercial I S P at the center of the Internet. It is well connected and has national and international coverage. From the slide list: A T and T in North America, Orange in Europe, and N T T or Tata Communications in Asia. The slide does not give the home regions.

38
00:09:02,090 --> 00:09:23,487
Question 28. An I X P is an Internet exchange point: a place where many I S Ps connect to each other and exchange packets. The slides do not list its hardware. This part is optional: in the textbook, an I X P has high speed switches that connect the routers of the I S Ps.

39
00:09:23,937 --> 00:09:28,332
Questions 29 to 31. Pause the video and try them.

40
00:09:28,782 --> 00:09:45,763
Question 29. Large content providers want to bring services and content close to the end users. At one I X P, they reach many I S Ps. So their traffic often bypasses the tier-1 and regional I S Ps.

41
00:09:46,213 --> 00:10:00,400
Question 30. A regional I S P connects the access networks of one region to larger I S Ps. It is the provider of the access networks and the customer of a larger I S P.

42
00:10:00,850 --> 00:10:27,175
Question 31 asks for two regional I S Ps in the Waterloo region. The slides do not name any, so this video does not answer that part. For the second part, use the slide definitions. A university network is an institutional access network, from slide 12. It connects its own hosts. So by the slides, it is an access network, not a regional I S P.

43
00:10:27,625 --> 00:10:44,009
Problem 2 of problem set 1. Users share a 2 Mbps link. Each user needs 300 kbps when it transmits, but it transmits only 12 percent of the time. Pause the video and try parts a to c.

44
00:10:44,459 --> 00:11:04,171
Part a. With circuit switching, each user reserves 300 kbps. 2000 divided by 300 is 6.67. Round down: 6 users. A seventh user would need 2100 kbps, which is more than 2000.

45
00:11:04,621 --> 00:11:12,024
Part b. A user transmits 12 percent of the time. So the probability is p equals 0.12.

46
00:11:12,474 --> 00:11:30,714
Part c. The users are independent. So the probability that exactly n of M users transmit is binomial. M choose n, times p to the power n, times q to the power M minus n, where q is 1 minus p, or 0.88.

47
00:11:31,164 --> 00:11:34,300
Parts d to f. Pause the video and try them.

48
00:11:34,750 --> 00:11:56,937
Part d. M is not given, so the answer is a formula in M. Use the complement. First find the probability that 6 or fewer users transmit: the sum of the binomial terms for k from 0 to 6. Then subtract it from 1. The solution also gives the direct form: the sum for k from 7 to M.

49
00:11:57,387 --> 00:12:19,488
Part e. With M equal to 10, add four terms. P of 7 is 2.93 times 10 to the power minus 5. P of 8 is 1.5 times 10 to the power minus 6. P of 9 and P of 10 are much smaller. The sum is about 3.08 times 10 to the power minus 5.

50
00:12:19,938 --> 00:12:34,999
Part f. The solution gives no number. It only says that the probability of 7 or more users is very small. So more than 6 users can share the link with statistical multiplexing, without much extra delay.

51
00:12:35,449 --> 00:12:59,663
This table is an added illustration, not part of the solution. It uses the formula of part d. For 20 users, the overload probability is about 0.0067. For 40 users, it is about 0.2, which is too large. A reasonable M is the largest M whose overload probability you accept.

52
00:13:00,113 --> 00:13:14,833
Problem 4 of problem set 1. Users share a 2 Mbps link. Each user transmits at 1 Mbps, but only 20 percent of the time. Pause the video and try parts a and b.

53
00:13:15,283 --> 00:13:24,307
Part a. Each user reserves 1 Mbps. 2 divided by 1 is 2. So circuit switching supports 2 users.

54
00:13:24,757 --> 00:13:44,533
Part b. If two or fewer users transmit, they need at most 2 Mbps. That is not more than the link rate, so there is essentially no queueing delay. If three users transmit, they need 3 Mbps. That is more than 2 Mbps. So packets queue before the link.

55
00:13:44,983 --> 00:13:48,161
Parts c and d. Pause the video and try them.

56
00:13:48,611 --> 00:13:54,649
Part c. A user transmits 20 percent of the time. So the probability is 0.2.

57
00:13:55,099 --> 00:14:20,293
Part d. The three users are independent. So the probability that all three transmit is 0.2 times 0.2 times 0.2, which is 0.008. The queue grows only when all three transmit. So the queue grows during 0.008 of the time, which is 0.8 percent. This matches the solution.

58
00:14:20,743 --> 00:14:27,719
Here is the recap. One. For circuit switching, divide the link rate by the user rate and round down.

59
00:14:28,169 --> 00:14:40,137
Two. For packet switching, use the binomial probability. The overload probability is the probability of more than k active users. Often the complement is easier.

60
00:14:40,587 --> 00:14:54,305
Three. Packet switching shares resources and needs no call setup, but congestion causes delay and loss. Circuit switching needs a setup and reserves resources, with guaranteed performance.

61
00:14:54,755 --> 00:15:14,787
Four. A full mesh of access I S Ps does not scale. So the Internet uses transit I S Ps, I X Ps, peering links, regional I S Ps and content provider networks, with tier-1 I S Ps at the center. That is the end of video 2 b.
